of a suite's own (`core_dir()`, `own_core()`, and their use in `tools/stage_app.py`, `tools/build_release.py` and
`tools/check_versions.py`), its Remote Play from the native wheels, and its mention in the README, the instruction
files and the Nexus page's Development chapter. Its wiki is no longer published under the Nexus page: it is that
repository's own. The BC250 Bazzite Suite's Gaming icon is that suite's silver controller since today (the gaming
apps' and the portal's CHANGELOGs); DESIGN.md says so.

Changelog: 13-11-2026 20:38:45

Release 8.0.0. Brings the new suites bazzite-console, windows-portable.

Changelog: 13-11-2026 20:18:54

Added. The Bazzite Console Suite (github.com/RobertoTorino/bazzite-console) uses the Nexus tools as the Windows
Portable Suite does: cloned into the Nexus checkout as `bazzite-console/` (in `.gitignore`, and in
`tools/suites.py`'s `OWN_REPOSITORIES`), its workflows starting with `.github/actions/suite`. A suite may carry a core
of its own, `<suite>/core/<package>/` with `<suite>/core/VERSION` (that suite's `bazzite_console_core`):
`tools/suites.py` has `core_dir()` and `own_core()`, `tools/stage_app.py` bundles the suite's core under its own
name, `tools/build_release.py` names it in the release notes and `tools/check_versions.py --suite` checks its
version. `tools/build_release.py` takes the native wheel of that suite's Remote Play. `docs.yml` builds the suite's
wiki at robertotorino.github.io/nexus/bazzite-console-suite/ once its repository is laid out for the Nexus checkout.
`tools/features.py` asks no column of a suite in a repository of its own, and `tools/ci-local.sh` leaves the suite's
folder out of its copy. The tests of the copies of core's modules compare only the apps on Nexus's core; the tests of
Nexus Dashboard and Nexus Installer Tests accept the suite when it is in the checkout.

